title: TreeRef forest
tags: [[Data model]]
kind: overview
aliases: tree forest treeref
summary: tree_ref fields place metarecords in trees — one forest per field name, no cycle, at most 1000 deep, one position per metarecord.

A `tree_ref` value places its metarecord in a tree: it names a ''parent'' (or none,
for a root) and the metarecord's ''name'' under it ([[Value types]]). All the
`tree_ref` rows of one field name form a ''forest'' — several roots allowed — and
different field names are independent forests: `mfr_path` is the filesystem, the
tag hierarchy is `path` on the tag entries, a collection could be another.

A forest is held to four rules on every write, each refused with `400`:

* ''no cycle'' — a node is never its own ancestor (`TreeRef write would create a cycle on 'path'`);
* ''at most 1000 levels'' (`TreeRef depth exceeds 1000`);
* ''unique siblings'' — one node per (parent, name) (`tree position already occupied for field 'path'`), so a path names one node;
* ''one position per metarecord'' ([[One position per forest]]), and a parent that exists in ''this'' forest, never taken away from under its children ([[Referential integrity of a forest]]).

Renaming a node changes its name; moving it changes its parent — both a `set` of its
one position, whatever the size of the subtree below it
([[The forest in the store]]).

A node's path is the names from its root down, joined by `/`
([[TreeRef path conventions]]); names are byte strings, shown as text
([[Tree names]]). Queries follow a forest with `->` and `->*`, and read a position's
parts with the `:parent`, `:path` aspects ([[Following references]], [[Field aspects]]).

